                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi r
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert y \vert \leq (1-c)r'
              and the bar means a closed ball. To find a fixed point, we use the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+fixed-point+theorem"} Banach fixed-point theorem
              and checking that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is a well-defined strict-contraction mapping is straightforward. Finally, we have:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi r
                    %mo ,
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi r
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f((-r, r)) \subset (-(1 + c)r, (1 + c)r)
            %p since
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ≤
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert f(x) \vert = \vert x + g(x) - g(0) \vert \le (1+c) \vert x \vert
            %p
          %p Now that we have established the lemma, we could proved the main theorem:
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              It is enough to prove the special case when
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
